meta {
  name: Ask before you move
  type: http
  seq: 63
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/timetables/{{timetableId}}/clash-check
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "rules": ["teacher", "room", "student_set"],
    "proposed": [
      {
        "ref": "maths-to-period-2",
        "replaces_timetable_slot_id": "{{slotId}}",
        "section_id": "{{sectionId}}",
        "period_id": "{{periodTwoId}}",
        "day_of_week": 1,
        "subject_offering_id": "{{offeringId}}",
        "teacher_id": "{{teacherId}}",
        "room_id": "{{labRoomId}}"
      }
    ]
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.clear: eq false
  res.body.count: eq 1
  res.body.clashes[0].rule: eq room
  res.body.clashes[0].right.ref: eq maths-to-period-2
}

docs {
  200, NOT 409. This is a report, not a refusal - it is what a timetabling
  screen calls while someone drags a class around, and answering 409 would
  make every honest question look like an error in the network tab.

  ref is yours: it comes back on the clashing side so a batch of proposals can
  be matched up to the rows on screen. replaces_timetable_slot_id excludes the
  slot being moved from the comparison, which is why moving maths onto its own
  period is not reported as colliding with itself.

  The one clash is on room: French already has the laboratory in period 2.
  Neither the teacher nor the student set collides, because French has nobody
  assigned to teach it and nobody actively enrolled in it by this point in the
  run - which is worth noticing, because it means a question that looks like
  it should clash three ways clashes once.

  from and to must be given together and turn on date-scoped checking, which
  walks the calendar and catches collisions that only deviations create.
  Without them only the weekly pattern is compared and dates_checked is null.
  At most 100 proposals in one call.
}
